google kubernetes engine or gke is  google's secured and fully managed  kubernetes service  that now includes a revolutionary  autopilot mode  it allows you to build deploy and run  fast reliable apps and services  but how do you ensure that the  infrastructure you provision to run  those services remains reliable  and available for developers today i'm  going to show you how to manage gke  infrastructure at scale with cloud  operations  welcome to engineering for reliability  with google cloud  imagine getting a ticket like this one  you know that a service is having an  issue  and the service owners are asking you to  help them determine whether it's being  caused by the gke infrastructure they're  running on  the problem is that there's a default  namespace and every cluster in your org  how can you possibly find the one you're  after let's see how cloud operations  can help from the monitoring overview  screen let's click on the gke dashboard  by looking at the last 6 hours of data  and expanding the alert timeline  we can in fact confirm that there are no  alerts in the default namespace  we can also confirm that there aren't  any issues with our nodes  let's have a look at the workloads in  that namespace to see if there are any  issues there  we can find the workload by clicking on  view all  and then searching for the namespace  where we know it's running  it looks like there is a memory  utilization alert for this workload  let's check to see if there are any  infrastructure issues that may be  causing this  let's click on metrics and select memory  under node metrics  here we can see that the nodes aren't  having any memory alerts or issues  so we can rule out infrastructure as a  probable cause for this particular  incident  and we can reply to the original message  letting them know that memory issues are  constrained to their specific service  being able to take a very limited amount  of information about a problem and  quickly determine whether an  infrastructure of issues at fault is  fantastic  as an operator your time is incredibly  valuable and being able to quickly  eliminate your infrastructure as a  culprit is  great but what about the service owner  let's have a look at another example  does your inbox look like this when  there's a problem of any kind  this might happen if there's an alert  flood caused by a widespread issue  or it might happen just because there  are a lot of alerts that might be  triggered for  any number of reasons how can you  quickly dig through this mess and figure  out what's actually important and  whether your service is impacted  let's see how cloud operations can help  let's see if we can figure out whether  our service was impacted by an  infrastructure issue  from the monitoring overview screen  let's go to the gke dashboard and have a  look at the behavior of our system over  the last day  when we expand the alerts timeline we  can see that there are two node alerts  let's have a look at the resource  associated with the alert  we can see that we are getting high cpu  alerts in the metrics tab we can confirm  elevated cpu utilization of the node  when we look at container metrics we can  see that there are a number of services  that  are running on this particular node some  of them did not see increased  utilization during interval  however at least two services did see  elevated cpus that corresponds to  elevated seputalization on the node  so we can confirm that not only was our  service impacted by increased resource  utilization of the node  but another service was as well  thanks for joining me today we saw how  cloud operations can help you quickly  isolate or eliminate infrastructure  issues from a  limited set of data we also saw how to  use the timeline to quickly filter  alerts  to determine whether your service is  having an issue or not  i hope this helps you to keep your gk  infrastructure reliable and available  thanks so much for watching don't forget  to like and subscribe to never miss out  on more  engineering for reliability with google  cloud see you soon
 

